WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.710
When you hear the word ‘Ectoplasm,’ your
first thought might be that one scene from

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:06.710
Ghostbusters.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:07.979
What happened?

00:00:07.979 --> 00:00:10.259
He slimed me.

00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:15.809
But the history of ectoplasm dates back to
the 19th and early 20th century, rising out

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:20.930
of the fascinating and often bizarre world
of séances.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:25.690
Attempts to communicate with the dead have
a long history, but the classic seance—with

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.859
the darkened room, the circle of people, and
the dead speaking through a medium—rose

00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:33.780
to prominence in the mid-nineteenth century.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.879
Interest died down, but then, in the 1920s,
after the First World War and 1918 flu outbreak,

00:00:39.879 --> 00:00:44.799
séances spiked like never before as survivors
tried to make sense of a world shattered by

00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:46.840
mass death and conflict.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.710
Struggling to process the loss of loved ones,
people increasingly turned to the world of

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:56.930
the paranormal as a source of comfort, hoping
to speak to deceased family members as a way

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.869
of processing their grief.

00:00:58.869 --> 00:01:02.960
But many weren’t content with just sitting
in a circle and having a medium claim they

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.000
could hear voices of the dead.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.320
Notable figures like British writer Sir Arthur
Conan Doyle, who wrote the Sherlock Holmes

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:14.460
stories and lost his son in the flu outbreak,
became increasingly interested in proving

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.190
the existence of spirits through physical
evidence.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:25.420
And so began a widespread fixation on scientifically
verifying a substance known as ectoplasm.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:32.790
Coming from the Greek ektos, meaning "outside",
and plasma, meaning "something formed or molded"

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:38.000
— ectoplasm was a gelatinous substance that
mediums claimed emerged from their mouth,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.820
nose (or other places) during seances.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.340
Some said it was a kind of connective tissue
between the world of the living and the dead.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:51.790
A nimble substance, it could (supposedly)
form into the body of the deceased, tapping

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.680
shoulders and playing musical instruments
and even, in the “spirit photography craze,”

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.390
popping into photos.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:03.510
Today, most of these photos appear… obviously
fraudulent, and in the early 20th century

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:07.540
researchers were already questioning the validity
of the images.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:13.670
They found many mediums used methods of swallowing
and regurgitating cloth or other textiles

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.410
to create the illusion of otherworldly substances
leaving their body.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:19.940
Pretty gross.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:26.370
Some photos of ectoplasm also depicted human
faces emerging from the ‘supposedly otherworldly’

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.459
substance.

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:29.630
Once again, some were fooled.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:34.220
Looking closely at the photographs, however,
investigators discovered the so-called faces

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.629
within the ectoplasm displayed the marks of
paper magazine cut-outs around their edges

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:41.500
— exposing the mediums as fake.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:47.250
Helen Duncan was perhaps the most famous medium
who claimed to produce ectoplasm — and the

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.100
last person to be imprisoned under the British
‘Witchcraft Act’ interestingly enough.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.849
She claimed spirits of the deceased materialized
from her mouth, which as you can tell by the

00:02:56.849 --> 00:02:59.700
photographs were fraudulent.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:05.410
Investigators discovered she made these so-called
specters from paper masks placed on sheets

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.599
draped over coat hangers.

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:12.709
Beyond spirits, Duncan also made claims of
a ghostly hand which emerged from her nose,

00:03:12.709 --> 00:03:18.680
which psychic researcher Harry Price concluded
was made of A: a woven cloth, and B: a rubber

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.220
glove.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.489
Researchers exposing ectoplasm as fraudulent
went beyond Duncan, however.

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:30.330
Indeed, the exposure of fake ectoplasm became
so widespread it resulted in a serious decline

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:34.050
in physical mediums — and the acceptance
of mediums in general.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:38.970
But people like Conan Doyle didn’t dupe
themselves en mass for no reason.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:43.800
Although it’s hard to deny these claims
of ectoplasm were fraudulent, they were responding

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.690
to a very real sense of pain and loss which
gripped the society.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:53.020
In suggesting the recently dead could still
be contacted, and indeed remained physically

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:59.160
present in some form, ectoplasm was, ultimately
a strange — yet important — stage in the

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.020
grieving process for many.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:07.020
Today, It’s easy to look at these photos
and laugh, or hear stories of people regurgitating

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:11.300
cloths and feel a little sick, but it’s
important to remember that in the wake of

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:15.240
a world-wide tragedy, people need something
tangible to hold on to.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:20.139
And strange though it might seem, in some
cases that was ghostly slime.

00:04:20.139 --> 00:04:26.639
And that’s the end of our tour of the mysterious,
tragic, and slightly disgusting history of

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:27.639
ectoplasm.

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:30.800
Thank you so much for watching this episode
of Curious Archive.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.139
If you enjoyed the content, please consider
subscribing and leaving this video a like.

00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:37.740
Thank you so much for watching, and I’ll
see you in the next video.

